#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124126 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/id #uuid "5c276425-f5ef-585f-9ab3-1b0262fff08e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12456" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2\\,$ on the ellipse $x^2 +4y^2-4=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 +4y^2 -4=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,8y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=8\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(4\\lambda -1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $x^2 +4y^2 -4  =0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,1 \\right), \\left(0,-1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4(1)-1)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2 +4y^2 -4  =0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(-2,0 \\right), \\left(2,0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0, \\pm 1), \\qquad (\\pm 2,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 1)$ | $(\\pm 2, 0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,\\pm 1),$ and its minimum value is $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124339 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/id #uuid "dc39fdca-dfbc-5806-b0aa-8480ee44b10f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8689" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y) = x+y\\,$ on the ellipse $x^2 + 2y^2 =6.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 + 2y^2 = 6.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=6\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda = 0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda\\neq 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{4\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y) = 6$ and solve for $\\lambda,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{2\\lambda })}^{2}+2\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4\\lambda })}^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{8{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2+1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx= \\pm 2 \\qquad y = \\pm 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(-2, -1 \\right), \\quad \\left(2, 1 \\right).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\, (x,y) \\,$ | $\\, \\left(-2, -1\\right) \\,$ | $\\, \\left(2, 1 \\right) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, f(x,y) \\,$ | $\\, -3 \\,$ | $\\, 3 \\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $\\left(2, 1 \\right),$ and its maximum value is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124342 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5328ab3-440f-5cc8-9b8f-e7f86a4dd740" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Three-Variable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "ebdcad27-1ee2-57e7-9ebf-29ccf0a8ad12" :question/math-academy-id "e-8736" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z) = x+y+z$ on the ellipsoid $x^2 + y^2 + 4z^2 = 36.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + 4z^2 = 36.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z), \\qquad g(x,y,z) = 36\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda = 0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\neq 0,$ we have the following:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/assets/0bae179a1a44fa0e659681ea8da4f648bc72b944ef6e2730079474dde5f141c3.png)\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\, y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z) = 36$ and solve for $\\lambda,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+4(\\frac{1}{64{\\lambda }^{2}}) & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{9}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =36 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\, y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx = \\pm 4 \\qquad y = \\pm 4 \\qquad z= \\pm 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-4, -4, -1),  \\qquad (4, 4, 1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\, (x,y,z) \\,$ | $\\, (-4, -4, -1) \\,$ | $\\, (4, 4, 1)  \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, f(x,y,z) \\,$ | $\\, -9 \\,$ | $\\, 9 \\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(4, 4, 1),$ and its maximum value is $9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e6eee64-c32e-5d16-b0f9-535d114b17ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367856} {:answer/id #uuid "5ffc5a10-1b94-545c-9675-fd6ce1559ec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367857} {:answer/id #uuid "caa3d1ee-30e1-55e0-a0de-ab75795f4e03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367858} {:answer/id #uuid "7a659e2d-ac51-58d9-9f6d-9302c0b642cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367859} {:answer/id #uuid "3aeeec3f-bbf0-57eb-9067-78cd7fe7fa74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aeeec3f-bbf0-57eb-9067-78cd7fe7fa74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367860} :answer-field/id #uuid "89288fdb-2fd0-53b8-b039-5ae5a78f7842" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7758e4d-f43a-51c7-9ad5-acad913aa1a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162760" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2\\,$ on the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=8\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1-4\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(1-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-8 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-2\\sqrt{2}),(0,2\\sqrt{2}).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1-\\frac{1}{4})=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-\\sqrt{2},0),(\\sqrt{2},0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0),\\,(0,\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ | $(0,\\pm 2\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $4$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ and its minimum value is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0aa7e37-9b21-548f-9632-dff256bd29a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121430} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe7c4e9-94ea-5933-b76c-cca336dede96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121431} {:answer/id #uuid "1c0d9444-d90f-54f6-b008-9c96f984608a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121432} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6099f0-6d05-5525-8c9e-5469786edb91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121433} {:answer/id #uuid "78c092c7-aa5e-5274-af00-06a2c60d318f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78c092c7-aa5e-5274-af00-06a2c60d318f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121434} :answer-field/id #uuid "d14f7371-c123-5b1d-b8c4-55e78dd983ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83f506ab-232e-59b7-85e6-ab9697a12cb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162762" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\,$ on the ellipse ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y+4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y+4=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(2\\lambda -1)=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-1),(0,1).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2(1)-1)=2\\,⇒\\,y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,-1),\\,(0,1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,-1)$ | $(0,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $(0,1)$ and its maximum value is $9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5328ab3-440f-5cc8-9b8f-e7f86a4dd740" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ce10b29-0826-53db-99f5-430727e7de83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367863} {:answer/id #uuid "d891f6cc-652b-5407-8ec4-ee5fc2c6c6e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367864} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a060a6-51ee-5899-bd86-92c6a9eafe7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367865} {:answer/id #uuid "d10cde52-5648-5ba6-b01a-c91ef0b9c7b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367866} {:answer/id #uuid "05b942fc-7b73-5503-aafa-3f613ec9de64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05b942fc-7b73-5503-aafa-3f613ec9de64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367867} :answer-field/id #uuid "280c962e-55a2-5680-a770-cd86f7699574" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7112b564-6c6b-5047-a472-e2f2bf2dd90c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162766" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=2x+y+5z$ on the ellipsoid $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}=38.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)=9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}=38.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=38\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,1,5⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨18x,2y,18z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=18\\lambda x \\\\ 1=2\\lambda y \\\\ 5=18\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{9\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{18\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=38$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2} & =38 \\\\ 9(\\frac{1}{81{\\lambda }^{2}})+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+9(\\frac{25}{324{\\lambda }^{2}}) & =38 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{25}{36{\\lambda }^{2}} & =38 \\\\ \\frac{19}{18{\\lambda }^{2}} & =38 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{36} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\,y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{2}{3},\\,y=\\pm 3,\\,z=\\pm \\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points \n\n$$\n(\\frac{2}{3},3,\\frac{5}{3}),\\,(-\\frac{2}{3},-3,-\\frac{5}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{2}{3},-3,-\\frac{5}{3})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{2}{3},3,\\frac{5}{3})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,-\\frac{38}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{38}{3}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{2}{3},3,\\frac{5}{3}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{38}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5328ab3-440f-5cc8-9b8f-e7f86a4dd740" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3459940-14c5-5535-b595-26b0c23242c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367870} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3de261-7c2f-5bfd-a041-eea56c4b35c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367871} {:answer/id #uuid "7cbe05b2-5fed-559a-b231-58f82de0d08c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367872} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca87d1f-c113-5860-8f00-cf3aefe0c552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367873} {:answer/id #uuid "50b414e6-8d3e-57c3-b58b-f3b6be1b879c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ca87d1f-c113-5860-8f00-cf3aefe0c552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367873} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf54a21a-9d5d-5d89-a00f-4e415a345cf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b04383ac-8d8b-5212-bad9-9688d9613be3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162767" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+y+4z$ on the ellipsoid $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=21.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=21.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=21\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2y,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=8\\lambda x \\\\ 1=2\\lambda y \\\\ 4=8\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{8\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ z=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=21$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =21 \\\\ 4(\\frac{1}{64{\\lambda }^{2}})+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+4(\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}) & =21 \\\\ \\frac{1}{16{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =21 \\\\ \\frac{21}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =21 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\,y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,y=\\pm 2,\\,z=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2},2,2),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},-2,-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},-2,-2)\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{2},2,2)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,-\\frac{21}{2}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{21}{2}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{1}{2},-2,-2),$ and its minimum value is $-\\frac{21}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfcdb557-b51d-5ccf-8b3c-0bbdca114d31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367877} {:answer/id #uuid "a853c2fe-69eb-5e70-81a5-93e741ce6f5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367878} {:answer/id #uuid "02417d08-371d-5c6b-992b-2027c76ea961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367879} {:answer/id #uuid "9c854f54-46ce-5e54-91fb-5673f16aa4a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367880} {:answer/id #uuid "788fa85b-f772-5627-90ed-7dddc74b444e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c854f54-46ce-5e54-91fb-5673f16aa4a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367880} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d898913-8006-5069-8b57-0a0e8188b42f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c7533c8-ffd4-5503-8fda-2861dc4dde8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162768" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=x-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,$ on the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1-2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,8y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1-2x=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=8\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}2x(1+2\\lambda )=1 \\\\ y(1-4\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-2 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-1,0),(1,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1+\\frac{1}{2})=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}),(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-1,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(\\frac{1}{3},\\pm \\frac{2}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(\\frac{1}{3},\\pm \\frac{2}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(-1,0)$ and its minimum value is $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37b2a6ee-3d6c-508b-9d4b-eabffdd354d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367884} {:answer/id #uuid "02b03ff4-5b32-54c1-ad2b-44dad19f0b17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367885} {:answer/id #uuid "463170cf-1d4f-5803-8d73-a0251596b15b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367886} {:answer/id #uuid "3d534c0d-8fe1-5f96-bdc0-827bbb724aed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367887} {:answer/id #uuid "0dab6631-745b-51c2-a547-904c4d8c1ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dab6631-745b-51c2-a547-904c4d8c1ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367888} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c143eab-df6b-56a1-88e8-1dd543ae6bed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8a469fe4-bd26-57e6-b2f9-8ccac040b897" :question/math-academy-id "q-162769" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=2x-{y}^{2}\\,$ on the hyperbola ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=3.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global maximum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the hyperbola.*" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2=-2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}1-x\\lambda =0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-3 & =0\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+x(-1)=0\\,⇒\\,x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-1,-2),(1,2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,-2),\\,(1,2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(1,-2)$ | $(1,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the points $(1,\\pm 2)$ and its maximum value is $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d77d207a-c8dc-5438-8337-d5e4c4fc4a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367891} {:answer/id #uuid "b00c29da-5503-5455-9ef5-41bb731b9d00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367892} {:answer/id #uuid "6b64bc24-0ba6-5bac-8cfc-c3bb8181ffc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367893} {:answer/id #uuid "d94165fb-050e-5014-aade-8bb547c69d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367894} {:answer/id #uuid "f1375675-5599-5f71-90d7-3ebbfabf5b94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d77d207a-c8dc-5438-8337-d5e4c4fc4a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367891} :answer-field/id #uuid "4acf2bec-5469-54a2-ba86-d634f0bcd6a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dfca6b1e-58fa-5598-a964-fcd2c70d8700" :question/math-academy-id "q-162770" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,$ on the parabola ${y}^{2}-4x=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={y}^{2}-4x=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-4,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=-4\\lambda  \\\\ -4y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=-2\\lambda  \\\\ y(\\lambda +2)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-4x=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x & =0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-2$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-2(-2)\\,⇒\\,x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-4x=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-16 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(4,-4),(4,4).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(4,\\pm 4).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(4,\\pm 4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-16$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(4,\\pm 4)$ and its minimum value is $-16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5328ab3-440f-5cc8-9b8f-e7f86a4dd740" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9daffb2-77fc-55b2-b5f9-ed7db4c685f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367898} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9695fd-f53a-5c62-833e-60ffcb2f6d88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367899} {:answer/id #uuid "dc483312-25b0-514d-a39e-20da2168e40a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367900} {:answer/id #uuid "c17cfee5-99a5-50a1-bc34-b44243ed2a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367901} {:answer/id #uuid "a248e921-81ef-5b48-a4d6-fe21277ed6ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a248e921-81ef-5b48-a4d6-fe21277ed6ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367902} :answer-field/id #uuid "15724dc1-3869-51f9-bb34-32ea40752b62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b00ca6d-1d8d-5276-bb46-aa10d6901ab8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162773" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ on the ellipsoid $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=36.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)=9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=36.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=36\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨18x,8y,2z⟩ \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨9x,4y,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=9\\lambda x \\\\ y=4\\lambda y \\\\ z=\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}(1-9\\lambda )x=0 \\\\ (1-4\\lambda )y=0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne \\frac{1}{9},$ $\\lambda \\ne \\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\lambda \\ne 1.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{9}.$ Then, by the second and third equations, we must have $y=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}=36\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0,0).$\n\n- Now, assume that $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}.$ Then, by the first and third equations, we must have $x=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n4{y}^{2}=36\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 3,0).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the first and second equations, we must have $x=y=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}=36\\,⇒\\,z=\\pm 6.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0,\\pm 6).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(\\pm 2,0,0)\\,$ | $\\,(0,\\pm 3,0)\\,$ | $\\,(0,0,\\pm 6)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,9\\,$ | $\\,36\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm 2,0,0),$ and its minimum value is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367903 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d01a309-4ffb-57e0-8a07-b04884399f9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367905} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f80dad-ac64-5141-9365-72f79ae41d25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367906} {:answer/id #uuid "513b2717-c86c-5202-8ed5-11c7e127b4b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367907} {:answer/id #uuid "e8bbfa91-bf43-53b3-bc49-e689903bae6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367908} {:answer/id #uuid "cb3be90c-3cba-5ee1-8315-47c52fd5e04a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "513b2717-c86c-5202-8ed5-11c7e127b4b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367907} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6d30036-482e-5781-bf58-183eb09d7518" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2e4a4e1c-5f89-5be5-9dd7-4fb3646b556e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162786" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=-2x+y-2\\,$ on the parabola ${x}^{2}-y=1.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}-y=1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ \\lambda =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=0$ and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y & =1 \\\\ 1-y & =1 \\\\ y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(1,0).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens upwards, there isn't a maximum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the minimum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(1,0)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(1,0),$ and its minimum value is $-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4747ee97-7353-565b-8665-ac5bc6afe5d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367912} {:answer/id #uuid "8d619399-a70a-5d5d-a1e5-42ffc65345e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367913} {:answer/id #uuid "06df1251-02dd-5268-877d-f6d84825cfd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367914} {:answer/id #uuid "d44922d6-11c7-539d-95a0-0997e8f394db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367915} {:answer/id #uuid "768349ba-ddb5-5741-952e-8fdb0032c122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d44922d6-11c7-539d-95a0-0997e8f394db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367915} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5f997d4-2bde-5cc6-a4a9-ee6045813feb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f05ecc04-c3b4-5c3e-9bbd-e2fed65356b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162791" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=-4x+y+4\\,$ on the parabola $8x-{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global maximum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=8x-{y}^{2}=1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8,-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4=8\\lambda  \\\\ 1=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2(-\\frac{1}{2})}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expression for $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1$ and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-{y}^{2}=1 \\\\ 8x-1 & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{4},1).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens to the right, there isn't a minimum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the maximum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{4},1)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{1}{4},1),$ and its maximum value is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a04ac0a-79c6-5b00-8574-00647393d45d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367919} {:answer/id #uuid "f6133b28-7f6c-5b86-bad2-c084215c6804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367920} {:answer/id #uuid "4360cc51-0574-52f4-a3b1-d659bd400801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367921} {:answer/id #uuid "c4dd6c0f-5434-50c2-9e9c-1c467c420215" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367922} {:answer/id #uuid "865e7df8-8cd0-585b-a9de-02ad745488f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6133b28-7f6c-5b86-bad2-c084215c6804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367920} :answer-field/id #uuid "aed646fd-7bd2-5321-9e1f-fba4a19f4b4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71dc08b4-34a9-5b5a-ba0b-3dfe03aa9398" :question/math-academy-id "q-99027" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=1-x-y\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-1=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,1+\\sqrt{2}\\,$ | $\\,1-\\sqrt{2}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),$ and its maximum value is $1+\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76f829ee-510b-5ce6-bec0-3d0e182d7d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367926} {:answer/id #uuid "4c444c5c-8d05-577b-b372-7314e3d24a07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367927} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4c7164-b46a-5adf-8e3e-c0320a4b107e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367928} {:answer/id #uuid "40ac8452-75a0-5316-b505-7db14a47b84e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367929} {:answer/id #uuid "f85aabbf-55f3-5537-9fba-a0a442f4a31f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76f829ee-510b-5ce6-bec0-3d0e182d7d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367926} :answer-field/id #uuid "46b78936-5559-5558-a9e0-e32cff132fd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f73e0bd-9235-515f-b460-d92ec101a4a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-99030" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=3x-y+6\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=10\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{3}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=10$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{10}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =10 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 3,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-3,1),\\,(3,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-3,1)\\,$ | $\\,(3,-1)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,16\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(3,-1),$ and its maximum value is $16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0ad433e-bf34-5472-afb4-a61f8aa558a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367933} {:answer/id #uuid "7d517a0a-2fba-597f-ac6e-0ea1992f6af0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367934} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0be87f-5c22-5950-9bd8-30a4b1a9ff2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367935} {:answer/id #uuid "163de838-dc37-5500-a65f-8e3c0bdf0920" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367936} {:answer/id #uuid "75419dfa-f953-5fc3-bf1e-38dba394828b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75419dfa-f953-5fc3-bf1e-38dba394828b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367937} :answer-field/id #uuid "a10838a4-04c1-5dcd-96e4-b4c4b7bf01cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e0d2180-89c7-5fc1-927b-204ba9c98d28" :question/math-academy-id "q-99039" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=4x+16y\\,$ on the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,16⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,8y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4=2\\lambda x \\\\ 16=8\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=4$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+4\\cdot \\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{4+16}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =4 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\,y=\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-8\\sqrt{5}\\,$ | $\\,8\\sqrt{5}\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),$ and its minimum value is $-8\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5328ab3-440f-5cc8-9b8f-e7f86a4dd740" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d6b7bc5-8058-5213-bf62-0d648a8998af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367940} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe688bb-4e1e-5571-912d-f6249ad68cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367941} {:answer/id #uuid "4157aea5-920d-5238-9c5a-9c33a268cab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367942} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2a79d4-4e03-5457-9414-8eb72a7c36f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367943} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a089e4-0b26-5536-82bb-3638741fa68a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e2a79d4-4e03-5457-9414-8eb72a7c36f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367943} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2cdb5c0-1855-5e4e-bcba-d8d41b4758c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "076baf6d-046b-5139-9903-a4aaa569fa9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-99168" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-yz$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-z,-y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -z=2\\lambda y \\\\ -y=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda -1)=0 \\\\ y(4{\\lambda }^{2}-1)=0 \\\\ z=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne 1$ and $\\lambda \\ne \\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the second and third equations, we must have $y=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(\\pm 1,0,0).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, by the first and third equations, we must have $x=0$ and $z=∓y.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+(-y)^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding values of $z$ are\n\n$$\nz=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\pm 1,0,0)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\,$ | $\\,∓\\frac{1}{2}\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the points $(\\pm 1,0,0),$  and its maximum value is $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5328ab3-440f-5cc8-9b8f-e7f86a4dd740" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de31e61c-2759-57ee-8a43-b41a7f1f8c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367947} {:answer/id #uuid "9823b2d3-0a8a-56a9-a263-c42741f78b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367948} {:answer/id #uuid "3c700f93-3d1e-5e3e-aa6a-7f34be5c44a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367949} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b45286-a8f6-5502-afeb-a8ea97469d14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367950} {:answer/id #uuid "25fc8253-7df5-55cd-afa1-2281c786cbab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de31e61c-2759-57ee-8a43-b41a7f1f8c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367947} :answer-field/id #uuid "977e5c07-dea6-5b83-a623-6c2d94830c24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f480ef2-819c-5f2d-b3f4-4140c7b1db3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99171" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=2x+6y+10z$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=35.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=35.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=35\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,6,10⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2\\lambda x \\\\ 6=2\\lambda y \\\\ 10=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{\\lambda } \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=35$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =35 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{25}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =35 \\\\ \\frac{35}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =35 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\,y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1\\,y=\\pm 3\\,z=\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-1,-3,-5),\\,(1,3,5).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(-1,-3,-5)\\,$ | $\\,(1,3,5)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,-70\\,$ | $\\,70\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,3,5),$ and its maximum value is $70.$"}]}
